1), W5 (1), W9 (1)\n- T2 (2), T3 (2), T9 (1)\n- B1 (1), B3 (2), B4 (1), B9 (2)\n\nSo the counts are:\n\n- W1:1, W5:1, W9:1\n- T2:2, T3:2, T9:1\n- B1:1, B3:2, B4:1, B9:2\n\nTo form seven pairs, I need seven pairs. Let's see:\n\n- T2:2 \u2192 1 pair\n- T3:2 \u2192 1 pair\n- B3:2 \u2192 1 pair\n- B9:2 \u2192 1 pair\n- W1:1, W5:1, W9:1, T9:1, B1:1, B4:1 \u2192 each is a single.\n\nSo that's four pairs. I need three more pairs. But I don't have three more pairs. The remaining tiles are singles. So Seven Pairs is not possible here.\n\nAnother pattern: All Terminals and Honors (32 points). But my hand has some non-terminal suited tiles (T2, T3, B3, B4). So no.\n\nMaybe All Chows (2 points). But my hand doesn't have four chows. Let's check:\n\nIn Bamboos: T2, T2, T3, T3, T9. Could form two chows if there are T4 and T5. But I don't have them. So no.\n\nAll Pungs (6 points): I don't have four pungs.\n\nFully Concealed Hand (4 points) is a given since no melds. Now, can I get other points?\n\nLooking at the hand, I have some terminals and some middle tiles. Maybe Outside Hand (4 points), which requires terminals or honors in every meld and pair. But since I have some non-terminal tiles (like T2, T3, B3, B4), this might not qualify.\n\nAlternatively, All Simples (2 points) if there are no terminals or honors. But I have W1, W9, T9, B1, B9, which are terminals. So no.\n\nWhat about One Voided Suit (1 point)? If I discard a tile from one suit to make two suits. For example, if I discard all Dots, but I have B1, B3, B4, B9. So not possible. Alternatively, if I discard a tile from one suit to have two suits. But I have three suits. So not applicable.\n\nClosed Wait (1 point) if I have only one tile to complete a pair, but since I'm discarding, maybe not. But I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can aim for a specific pattern. Let's see. For example, if I can form a hand with three pungs and a pair. Let's see:\n\nIn Dots: B1, B3, B3, B4, B9, B9. If I can get a B2 and B5 to form sequences, but I don't have them. Alternatively, if I can get another B3 to make a pung. Since there are four B3s in the unshown, if I discard a B3, maybe someone else can take it, but I need to keep my hand concealed. So maybe it's better to keep both B3s.\n\nIn Bamboos: T2, T2, T3, T3, T9. If I can get a T4 and T5 to form two chows. But again, not in my hand.\n\nCharacters: W1, W5, W9. Not helpful.\n\nSo perhaps my best bet is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential pairs or sequences. For example, W5 is a single tile that's not part of any sequence. Discarding it would not affect any pairs or sequences. Alternatively, B4 is a single tile. Let's compare:\n\nIf I discard W5, the unshown W5 has 3 left. If I discard B4, the unshown B4 has 4 left. So discarding W5 might be less risky because there's a higher chance someone else might need it. But since my hand is concealed, maybe it's better to discard W5 to open up more possibilities for my hand.\n\nWait, but I have a concealed hand. If I discard W5, and someone else takes it to form a meld, that's okay. But I need to keep my hand as concealed as possible. Alternatively, maybe discarding B4 is better. Let's see.\n\nAnother angle: the valid actions include Play B4. If I discard B4, then my Dots suit has B1, B3, B3, B9, B9. The B4 is gone. So the Dots could have a sequence B1-B2-B3 if I can get B2. But without B2, it's not possible. Alternatively, B3-B4-B5 if I have B5. But I don't have B5. So discarding B4 might not affect much.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W5, the Characters suit has W1 and W9 left. So maybe it's better to keep the W5 for possible future use, but since I can't form anything with it, it's better to discard.\n\nConsidering all, the best tile to discard might be W5, as it's a middle tile not part of any sequence, and discarding it doesn't break any potential pairs. Also, since I have two T2s and two T3s, keeping those pairs might be more valuable. So I'll go with discarding W5.\n", "output":