w. Maybe I need to think differently.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for the All Simples pattern? But All Simples requires no terminals or honors. I have several terminals and a J2, so that's not possible.\n\nWhat about All Chows? All Chows is 2 points. Let's see. If I can form four chows and a pair. For example:\n\n- T2, T3, T4 (but no T4)\n- T2, T3, T2? No.\n- B1, B3, B4? Not consecutive.\n- W1, W9, maybe not. \n\nThis seems difficult. Maybe I should focus on discarding tiles that are not useful and see if I can form a better hand.\n\nLooking at my valid actions: Play B9, T9, W9, T3, B1, J2, B3, W1, T2, B4.\n\nIf I discard B9, that's a duplicate in the unshown tiles (B9 has 4 left). But I have two B9s. If I discard one, maybe I can get rid of a high tile. Alternatively, if I discard T9, which is a terminal. But T9 is a single. If I discard T9, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But I need to think about my own hand.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a pair of T2s and T3s. If I have T2x2 and T3x2, maybe I can form two pairs. Then, with B3x2, B9x2, and J2x1, W1, W9, T9, B1. That's four pairs and three singles. Not enough for Seven Pairs. But if I can form a pair with one of the singles by drawing a tile.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. For example, J2 is a single. If I discard J2, maybe I can reduce the number of non-terminal/honor tiles. Let's see. If I discard J2, then my hand has W1, W9, T2x2, T3x2, T9, B1, B3x2, B4, B9x2. Now, I have more terminals and maybe can aim for All Terminals and Honors if I can get rid of T2, T3, B4. But that's not possible now.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard B1. B1 is a terminal, but I have only one. If I discard B1, then I can focus on other tiles. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, the unshown tiles show that B5 is 0, so no B5 left. The previous player has a meld of B5 pung. So I can't get B5. \n\nLooking at the game history, the previous player has played W3, W4, W5, and B5 pun