y have a third T2, they can make a pung. But they don't. So that's not possible. \n\nTherefore, Penging W8 might not be beneficial. It would create an exposed pung, but the rest of the hand is hard to form. \n\nAnother angle: the user's current hand has no melds. If they keep it concealed, they can aim for Fully Concealed (4 points) and maybe All Chows (if they can form four chows and a pair). Let's see: \n\nTheir hand is W1, W2, W8x2, T2x2, T3x2, T8, T9, B1, B3x2. \n\nLooking for chows: \n\n- W1 and W2: need a W3 for a chow. They don't have it.\n- T2 and T3: need a T4. Not present.\n- T8 and T9: need a T7. Not present.\n- B3x2: need a B2 or B4. Not in hand.\n\nSo it's hard to form chows. Maybe they can form pungs. They have two T2s and two T3s. If they can get another T2 or T3, they can make pungs. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a Seven Pairs. They have pairs in W8 (two), T2 (two), T3 (two), B3 (two). That's four pairs. They need three more pairs. They have W1, W2, T8, T9, B1. So maybe pair W1 with W2? No, they are different. So Seven Pairs might not be possible. \n\nIf they keep the hand concealed, the best they can do is maybe get Fully Concealed (4 points) and some others like All Simples (if no terminals or honors). Let's see: \n\nTheir hand includes W1 (terminal), W9 (but they have W8, not W9), T8 and T9 (terminals). So they have terminals, so All Simples (2 points) is not applicable. \n\nThey have no honor tiles in their hand (F1, F2, etc. are not in their hand). So No Honors (1 point) could be counted. \n\nFully Concealed (4 points) and No Honors (1 point) would give 5 points, which is below the 8-point minimum to win. So they need to find other patterns. \n\nIf they can form a hand with multiple pungs. For example, if they have two pungs, that's All Pungs (6 points). But with their current hand, they have two T2s and two T3s. If they can get another T2 or T3, they can make two pungs. \n\nBut since they can't Peng, maybe they should discard a tile to